Q: Android Studio: How can I see sources for android support v4 and v7? A: <p>Following from the above research done by Paul Verest...</p>

<p>IDE: Android studio 1.3.2</p>

<p>It is a 2-step process: Consider this sample build.gradle</p>

<p>1) Add the following to your build.gradle (Module:app) - search the 2 //Add comments below.</p>

<pre><code>apply plugin: 'com.android.application'
apply plugin: 'idea' //Add

android {
  compileSdkVersion 21
  buildToolsVersion "21.1.2"

  defaultConfig {
      applicationId "com.mycompany.android.myapp"
      minSdkVersion 16
      targetSdkVersion 21
      versionCode 1
      versionName "1.0"
  }
  buildTypes {
      release {
          proguardFiles getDefaultProguardFile('proguard-android.txt'),   'proguard-rules.pro'
      }
  }
}

dependencies {
    compile fileTree(dir: 'libs', include: ['*.jar'])
    compile 'com.android.support:appcompat-v7:22.1.0'
}

//Add
idea {
      module {
          downloadJavadoc = true
          downloadSources = true
      }
  }
</code></pre>

<p>2) Rebuild project.</p>

<p>After this point if you want to see source in Android Studio, it will pull up the source *.java instead of decompiled *.class</p>

Q: Using SystemProperties to get Serial (Brand, Device, etc.) on Android A: <p>As Hacketo linked to <em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2641111/where-is-android-os-systemproperties">Where is android.os.SystemProperties?</a></em></p>
<p><code>android.os.SystemProperties</code> is hidden, with a nonpublic API.</p>
<p>That can be seen at the raw version at <a href="http://grepcode.com/file_/repository.grepcode.com/java/ext/com.google.android/android/2.3_r1/android/os/SystemProperties.java/?v=source" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://grepcode.com/file_/repository.grepcode.com/java/ext/com.google.android/android/2.3_r1/android/os/SystemProperties.java/?v=source</a></p>
<p>One of the ways to call it (though it may not be available in some versions) is using reflection.</p>
<p>So the code is like:</p>
<pre><code>    String serial = null;
    if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT &gt;= Build.VERSION_CODES.GINGERBREAD) {
        serial = Build.SERIAL;
    } else {
        try {
             Class&lt;?&gt; c = Class.forName(&quot;android.os.SystemProperties&quot;);
             Method get = c.getMethod(&quot;get&quot;, String.class);
             serial = (String) get.invoke(c, &quot;ro.serialno&quot;);
         } catch (Exception ignored) {
         }
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Can font-size be set by unicode range? A: <p>Interestingly enough, you got it almost done yourself already; the <code>size-adjust</code> with the <code>unicode-range</code> cherry picking <em>does</em> seem to work for the <code>@font-face</code>. The only detail you have to watch carefully is the postscript identifier in the <code>local()</code> addressing part, and the actual Unicode codepoints.</p>
<p>POC using single &quot;franken-font&quot; family (multiple faces dealing with different ranges sharing the same name) where basis is a Cascadia Mono with additional enlarged vowels (and also &quot;bolded&quot; through the &quot;wght&quot; axis) and with one Emoji from a different font that is also &quot;inflated&quot;. Final Emoji will be rendered in normal size, by whatever font given browser finds suitable:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@font-face
{
 font-family: C;
 src: local('CascadiaMono-regular'), url('https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/cascadiamono/v5/TUZ2zw5pquJF3iuizJDZYqr1aZIn0A.woff2');
 font-variation-settings: "wght" 200;
}
@font-face
{
 font-family: C;
 src: local('CascadiaMono-regular'), url('https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/cascadiamono/v5/TUZ2zw5pquJF3iuizJDZYqr1aZIn0A.woff2');
 unicode-range: U+61, U+65, U+69, U+6f, U+75, U+79;
 /* aeiouy */
 font-variation-settings: "wght" 600;
 size-adjust: 200%;
}
@font-face
{
 font-family: C;
 src: local('Twemoji Mozilla'), url('https://xem.github.io/unicode13/Twemoji.ttf');
 unicode-range: U+1F984;
 /* 🦄 */
 size-adjust: 200%;
}
html { font-family: C; color-scheme: dark light; text-align: center; }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;p&gt;abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz🦄💩</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>This should work in Firefox (it has baked-in Emoji font <code>Twemoji Mozilla</code>) on Windows (comes (or may come) with the Cascadia font) without network.  Network fallbacks added to make it interoperable, but cannot guarantee they will hold infinitely.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/DJeSjH4E.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/DJeSjH4E.png" alt="Lower case alphabet with &quot;inflated&quot; vowels and an unicorn emoji (also inflated) and finally pile of poo emoji, in a regular size." /></a></p>
<h3>Notes:</h3>
<h4>Why &quot;franken-font&quot;</h4>
<p>This approach seems sightly more convenient than fabricating fallback stack each time; it is actually the intended way. Compare</p>
<pre><code>font-family: &quot;Enlarged vowels&quot;, &quot;Rest of Latin&quot;, &quot;Enlarged Emoji&quot;, &quot;Rest of Emoji&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>where each family name would have single <code>@font-face { font-family: …; etc… }</code> with</p>
<pre><code>font-family: &quot;Franken font&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>where all faces belong to single superfamily and individual faces claim distinct styles, weights, stretches and ranges each: <code>@font-face { font-family: &quot;Franken font&quot;; unicode-range: …; etc… }</code>.</p>
<h4>How to identify which (possibly local) font supports what characters and how to address it with the <code>local()</code> identifier</h4>
<p>Let's have a sample document where all font choices are let on the browser defaults:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;p&gt;&amp;#x1F1E8;&amp;#x1F1ED; &amp;#x1F984; &amp;#x0159;
&lt;p&gt;🇨🇭 🦄 ř
</code></pre>
<p>(Both paragraphs contain exact same Unicode characters)
Inspecting that document with devtools should reveal the actual used fonts; for example Firefox has a dedicated pane for that:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/HltjbWGO.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/HltjbWGO.png" alt="Fragment of Firefox devtools with Fonts pane active, listing three fonts in picked element: Segoe UI Emoji (highlighted), Twemoji Mozilla, and Verdana. On the right there is the rendered document with three glyphs: Flag of Switzerland, unicorn (highlighted), and Latin letter &quot;r&quot; with caron." /></a></p>
<p>That reveals the names of all font present in the selected element (and even in entire document -- not pictured). Second repeated names is the actual &quot;fullName&quot; that for system fonts can be used in the <code>local()</code> function.</p>
<p>For verifying what glyphs given font actually supports (any many more details) I'd recommend <a href="https://wakamaifondue.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">wakamaifondue.com</a>: for example locating the font file from the <code>&lt;Firefox install directory&gt;\fonts\TwemojiMozilla.ttf</code> and throwing it at this web-app will also reveal the <code>fullName</code> (<em>Twemoji Mozilla</em>), also the <code>PostScript name</code> (<em>TwemojiMozilla</em> - this is <strong>also</strong> valid <code>local()</code> identifier)</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/2fuxGbZM.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/2fuxGbZM.png" alt="" /></a></p>
<p>you can browse all glyphs and try Open Type features, and finally you can conveniently get the range(s) in the &quot;CSS&quot; section with additional options checked:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/JpaQFlk2.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/JpaQFlk2.png" alt="" /></a></p>
<p>(Side note: this range is correct, but due to the inherent bug in this font range for digits shouldn't be there, since it's claimed codepoints were actually left blank.)</p>
